<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Mosque of Tlemcen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1136</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> is the principal mosque of Tlemcen in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, founded under the Almoravid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> and rebuilt and enlarged under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-yusuf">Ali ibn Yusuf</a> in 1136. Its ribbed dome before the mihrab, with interlacing arches inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a>, is one of the finest works of Almoravid architecture. The Zayyanid ruler Yaghmurasen added the minaret in the thirteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-tlemcen.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of Tlemcen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BRAHIM DJELLOUL Mustapha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_mosqu%C3%A9e_et_d%C3%A9pendance_Minaret_de_la_Mosqu%C3%A9e_003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
